No separate pipeline is required to check for drift. Running a terraform plan within your existing pipeline is sufficient. Each time you execute plan before apply, you gain visibility into the proposed changes—whether resources will be added, modified, or destroyed. Reviewing this output ensures you maintain control over the infrastructure state.

If manual changes have been introduced, the plan will highlight potential destruction or mismatches. In such cases, you must incorporate those changes back into your Terraform code to keep everything consistent. Best practice dictates that no manual modifications should be made directly to the infrastructure; all changes should flow through Terraform.

For urgent resource creation, you can provision manually if absolutely necessary, but the next pipeline run must include the corresponding Terraform code. Adding an explicit block for that resource ensures your cloud environment stays in sync with your Terraform configuration


